# Coding Conventions

These conventions apply to all implementation work in this project. Agents
performing implementation (dev, code-reviewer, QA) must read this file
before writing or reviewing code. Planning-only sessions do not need to
load this file.

---

## General Development

- Write tests for all new functionality before marking a task complete.
- Run the project's lint and test suite before marking a task complete.
- When using `try/except` or equivalent error handling for graceful degradation,
  the catch clause must include at minimum a log statement identifying what was
  attempted and which fallback was triggered. Silent exception swallowing makes
  production triage impossible.
- Before submitting a story that includes rename or refactor work for code
  review, the dev agent must grep the old identifier across all files modified
  in the story and verify zero matches remain in production code. **Evidence
  required:** Log the grep command and its output in the story file under
  "Rename Verification". Zero matches required. Gate validation will check
  for this section.
- Commit messages follow conventional commits with scope: `feat(scope):`,
  `fix(scope):`, `docs(scope):`, `test(scope):`. Always include the scope
  parenthetical.
- One story per commit. Do not batch multiple stories into a single commit.
- Before committing any story, the dev agent must run `git diff --staged` and
  verify every changed file and line is within the story's stated scope.
  Out-of-scope changes must be stashed or committed separately. **Evidence
  required:** Log `git diff --staged --stat` output in the story file under
  "Scope Verification".
- Configure pre-commit secret scanning. Do not rely on code review as the
  sole defense against credential leaks.
- When adding new environment variables, update the real `.env` and any
  environment template file in the same commit.
- Post-gate hotfixes that change production behavior must include a test case
  in the same commit or the immediately following commit. Do not defer hotfix
  test coverage to the next sprint.
- When live deployment fixes modify response shapes, UI labels, or DOM
  structure, the same commit (or immediately following commit) must update
  all affected test fixtures and assertions.

