## Smoke Test Maintenance (Hard Gate)

Smoke tests validate the live deployed instance end-to-end. They are
the last automated check before the human sees the production build.
**Smoke tests must pass for deployment to proceed** — a failing smoke
test blocks the Production Validation Checkpoint.

### Coverage requirement

Every story that introduces, modifies, or removes user-facing
functionality MUST include corresponding smoke test updates **in the
same commit**. Specifically:

- **New feature:** Add a smoke test that exercises the feature's
  primary path against the live deployment.
- **Modified behavior:** Update existing smoke tests to reflect the
  changed behavior. Verify the test would have failed against the old
  behavior to confirm it is actually testing the change.
- **Removed feature:** Remove or update smoke tests that reference
  the removed functionality. Stale smoke tests that fail on deploy
  are as harmful as missing ones.
- **API changes:** If the story changes an API response shape, update
  smoke tests that consume that endpoint.

### Test type must match change type

Smoke tests must verify the change **at the layer the user experiences
it**. An API health check does not constitute smoke coverage for a UI
change. Match the test type to the change:

| Change Type | Required Smoke Test |
|---|---|
| New/modified UI component | Browser test (e.g., Playwright) that verifies the component renders with production data |
| New/modified API endpoint | HTTP test that verifies the endpoint returns the expected response shape |
| New/modified computed value | Live test that fetches and independently verifies the computation |
| Changed display formatting | Browser test that reads the rendered value and verifies formatting |
| Removed feature | Verify the removed surface/endpoint is no longer accessible |

**API-returns-200 is not smoke coverage for a UI change.** If a story
adds a visible UI element, the smoke test must open a browser, navigate
to the page, and verify the element renders. If the project has an
existing browser test framework (e.g., Playwright), new UI smoke tests
must use it.

### What smoke tests are NOT

Smoke tests are not unit tests. They run against the live deployed
instance, not a dev server or mock. They verify that the deployment
succeeded and critical paths work end-to-end. They run in seconds
(not minutes) and cover the happy path plus critical error cases.

### Evidence

Log smoke test updates in the story file under "Smoke Test Updates":
- Which test files were added/modified/removed
- What functionality the test covers
- **What layer the test verifies** (browser, API, computation)
- Confirmation the test passes against the deployed instance

QA will reject stories that modify deployed functionality without
corresponding smoke test updates. **QA must also verify the test type
matches the change type** — an API test for a UI change is insufficient
and must be rejected. Code review will classify missing or mismatched
smoke test updates as Important severity.

